  "body": {
    "id": "prop:contrast-extinction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(h_0\\in(0,\\infty)\\), the absolute \\(\\Lambda\\)-mass of the propensity-path component is \\(\\int |h_0u/(1+h_0s)^2|\\,d\\Lambda=\\alpha\\operatorname{Beta}(1+\\kappa,1-\\kappa)E_H|u|\\,h_0^{-\\kappa}\\). Hence this component is absent at a finite critical coordinate if and only if \\(u=0\\) \\(H\\)-almost surely; if \\(E_H|u|>0\\), it remains strictly positive for every finite \\(h_0\\) and vanishes only at the stable endpoint \\(h_0\\to\\infty\\). Moreover \\(\\int|g_{h_0}(s,r,u)-r/s|\\,d\\Lambda=O(h_0^{-\\kappa})\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "hlimit",
      "H",
      "gh",
      "Lambda",
      "kappa",
      "alpha"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:critical-law",
      "def:stable-law",
      "thm:oracle-hardening-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "This prevents invalid residual-only critical calibration and identifies precisely when the extra causal-policy mark can be ignored.",
    "gap": "Neither Kennedy2019Incremental nor the scalar boundary laws in MaWang2020RobustIPW characterize equality of these marked Lévy pushforwards.",
    "consumer": "Software can use the criterion to decide whether a simpler stable-mark approximation is legitimate at a finite hardening coordinate.",
    "proof_tex": "The propensity-path part of \\(g_{h_0}\\) is \\(h_0u/(1+h_0s)^2\\).  Tonelli's theorem applies because its absolute value is nonnegative, and the bounded support of \\(H\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n \\int\\left|\\frac{h_0u}{(1+h_0s)^2}\\right|d\\Lambda\n &=\\alpha E_H|u|\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{h_0s^\\kappa}{(1+h_0s)^2}\\,ds\\\\\n &=\\alpha E_H|u|h_0^{-\\kappa}\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{v^\\kappa}{(1+v)^2}\\,dv\\\\\n &=\\alpha\\operatorname{Beta}(1+\\kappa,1-\\kappa)E_H|u|h_0^{-\\kappa}.\n\\end{split} \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe beta integral is finite and strictly positive because \\(0<\\kappa<1\\).  Thus (1) is zero exactly when \\(E_H|u|=0\\), equivalently when \\(u=0\\) \\(H\\)-almost surely.  If \\(E_H|u|>0\\), it is positive at every finite \\(h_0\\), and it tends to zero exactly along \\(h_0\\to\\infty\\).\n\nFor the complete jump map,\n\\[\n g_{h_0}(s,r,u)-\\frac rs\n =-\\frac{r}{s(1+h_0s)}+\\frac{h_0u}{(1+h_0s)^2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nUsing (2), Tonelli, and \\(|r|,|u|\\leq2B\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n \\int\\left|g_{h_0}-\\frac rs\\right|d\\Lambda\n &\\leq \\alpha E_H|r|\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{s^{\\kappa-1}}{1+h_0s}\\,ds\n +\\alpha E_H|u|\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{h_0s^\\kappa}{(1+h_0s)^2}\\,ds\\\\\n &=h_0^{-\\kappa}\\alpha\\!\\left[\n E_H|r|\\operatorname{Beta}(\\kappa,1-\\kappa)\n +E_H|u|\\operatorname{Beta}(1+\\kappa,1-\\kappa)\n \\right]\\\\\n &=O(h_0^{-\\kappa}).\n\\end{split}\n\\]\nThis proves both the exact mass formula and the hard-endpoint extinction rate.",
